  <h2>1. How this page relates to the Terms of Service</h2>
  <p>
    This page is DaoismHub's compliance statement. It records the editorial standards, the
    evidence discipline, the health-claim policy, the handling of practitioner experience
    records, the copyright and security procedures, and the independence commitments under
    which the sites are published. It applies to the DaoismHub knowledge base at
    <strong>daoismhub.com</strong> ("the main site") and the structured data registry at
    <strong>data.daoismhub.com</strong> ("the Data Registry"), operated together as a single
    editorial project ("DaoismHub", "we", "us").
  </p>
  <p>
    The binding legal terms governing your use of the sites are set out separately in the
    <a href="/terms-of-service/">Terms of Service</a>, and should be read with the
    <a href="/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a>, the <a href="/cookies/">Cookie Policy</a> and the
    <a href="/disclaimer/">Disclaimer</a>. The sites are intended for a general adult
    readership and are not directed at children under 16.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Nature of the content</h2>
  <p>
    DaoismHub is an English-language educational reference about Chinese contemplative
    traditions — Daoism, Chan Buddhism and related self-cultivation — covering texts,
    history, concepts, practices, modern research and anonymized practitioner experience.
    All content is editorial and educational. Nothing on these sites is medical,
    psychological, legal, financial or religious-instructional advice, and nothing creates a
    professional relationship. See the <a href="/disclaimer/">Disclaimer</a> for the full
    scope and its limits, including practice-safety warnings.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Intellectual property and permitted reuse</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Our content.</strong> Unless otherwise stated, the original text, structure,
      English translations, data schemas, knowledge-graph records, page design and code of
      both sites are owned by DaoismHub and protected by copyright and, where applicable,
      database rights. All rights reserved.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Classical sources.</strong> The classical Chinese texts referred to (Daodejing,
      Zhuangzi and others) are in the public domain in their original form. English
      translations published here are original editorial renderings and are covered by this
      site's copyright.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Permitted use.</strong> You may read the sites; quote short passages for
      private study, teaching, research, review or commentary; and link to any page — provided
      you credit DaoismHub by name and link to the source page. Quotation should be no more
      than necessary for the purpose, and must not imply that we endorse you, your product or
      your interpretation.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Not permitted without written permission.</strong> Republishing substantial
      portions of either site; mirroring or cloning the sites; translating and republishing
      whole pages; selling, licensing or bundling our content; using our content to train a
      commercial model where the output substitutes for the site; or presenting our text as
      your own.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Machine-readable data.</strong> Structured datasets listed on the
      <a href="/data/">Data page</a> are published for research and interoperability.
      Attribution is required for any reuse or derivative dataset, and the schema and field
      definitions should be cited alongside the data.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Requests.</strong> For permission beyond the above — syndication, print,
      translation, commercial licensing — email us; we answer permission requests and are
      usually open to reasonable scholarly and educational use.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>4. Acceptable use</h2>
  <p>You agree not to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>use the sites in violation of applicable law, or to infringe the rights of others;</li>
    <li>attempt to gain unauthorised access to the hosting infrastructure, or probe, scan or test its security without permission;</li>
    <li>interfere with the availability of the sites, including by flooding, denial-of-service or resource-exhaustion requests;</li>
    <li>scrape, mirror or bulk-download the sites at a rate or scale that degrades service for others;</li>
    <li>remove or obscure attribution, copyright notices or source citations;</li>
    <li>misrepresent the content, or present our editorial material as your own or as professional advice;</li>
    <li>use content from the sites to make medical, therapeutic, psychological or financial claims, or to promote a product, service or practitioner;</li>
    <li>use the sites in a way that suggests endorsement by DaoismHub of you or your organisation.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>5. Automated access, crawling and AI systems</h2>
  <p>
    We want the reference to be findable and usable by readers wherever they search,
    including through AI assistants and retrieval systems. Automated access is therefore
    permitted within the following limits:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Search engines and AI crawlers</strong> may crawl and index the sites for the
      purpose of returning readers to the source. Our <a href="/robots.txt">robots.txt</a>
      states the crawlers we explicitly welcome.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Retrieval and citation</strong> — quoting or summarising a page in an answer,
      with a link to the source — is welcome, and is the use the site is built for.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Bulk republication is not permitted</strong>: reproducing the site's pages in
      full, or in substantial part, in another publication, dataset or model output that
      substitutes for the site, requires written permission.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Rate limits.</strong> Crawlers should respect reasonable request rates, honour
      caching headers, and identify themselves honestly in the user agent.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No circumvention.</strong> Do not attempt to bypass access controls, rate
      limits or security measures.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If you operate a crawler and are unsure whether your use falls within this statement or
    the <a href="/terms-of-service/">Terms of Service</a>, write to us before crawling at
    scale. We would rather grant permission than block a legitimate index.
  </p>

  <h2>6. Corrections and submissions</h2>
  <p>
    Corrections, source suggestions and collaboration enquiries are welcome through the
    <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>. If you send us material:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>you confirm you have the right to send it and that it does not infringe anyone else's rights;</li>
    <li>you grant us a non-exclusive, royalty-free licence to use, edit, condense, translate and publish it in connection with the sites;</li>
    <li>we are not obliged to publish or keep any submission, and may edit for accuracy, length, tone and house style;</li>
    <li>we do not accept confidential material — anything you send may be treated as non-confidential;</li>
    <li>for practitioner experience reports, we require that the material be your own account, or be drawn from a public discussion with identifying details removed, and that no third party be identifiable.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>7. Editorial standards and independence</h2>
  <p>
    DaoismHub publishes under an explicit evidence discipline. Primary sources, scholarly
    interpretation, practitioner reports and editorial analysis are kept distinct and are
    never conflated; every content page carries an evidence class. Claims about research
    report what studies show, in what populations, and what they do not prove. Where
    evidence is thin, the site says so. Where traditions disagree, the site records the
    disagreement rather than manufacturing a consensus. The full classification system is
    documented in the
    <a href="https://data.daoismhub.com/schemas/evidence-system/">Evidence System</a> and the
    <a href="https://data.daoismhub.com/schemas/data-schema/">Data Schema</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The site carries no advertising, no sponsored content, no affiliate links and no paid
    placements. No tradition, teacher, organisation or commercial interest has editorial
    control over what is published, and no payment is accepted for coverage, ranking or
    inclusion. Corrections are made on the merits, and are dated.
  </p>

  <h2>8. Health-claim compliance</h2>
  <p>
    DaoismHub makes no diagnosis, treatment, cure or prevention claims of any kind, and does
    not market any practice, product or service as a therapy. Meditation and contemplative
    research is discussed <em>as research</em> — with methods, sample sizes and limitations
    stated — and never as medical endorsement. Descriptions of traditional practice are
    historical and ethnographic, not recommendations. Practice-safety warnings are set out
    in the <a href="/disclaimer/">Disclaimer</a> and should be read before attempting
    anything described on the site.
  </p>

  <h2>9. Practitioner experience records</h2>
  <p>
    The experience collection documents anonymized, paraphrased reports drawn from public
    community discussions, together with a small number of first-person reflections by the
    site's editor published under the editor's own byline. No usernames, locations or identifying details are retained, and
    identifying details are removed or generalised. These records are presented as
    practitioner self-reports only — never as evidence of efficacy or safety, never as
    clinical data, and never as a recommendation. They are not a substitute for professional
    advice.
  </p>

  <h2>10. Third-party links</h2>
  <p>
    Both sites link to external resources — for example ctext.org for classical texts, or a
    publisher for a cited paper. We do not control third-party sites and are not responsible
    for their content, accuracy, policies or availability. A link is a citation of record,
    not an endorsement, and does not imply any affiliation.
  </p>

  <h2>11. Copyright and infringement complaints</h2>
  <p>
    If you believe content on either site infringes your rights, please email us with:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>the URL of the page you object to;</li>
    <li>identification of the work you say is infringed, and your basis for holding the rights;</li>
    <li>the specific passage or element you object to, and why;</li>
    <li>your name, address and contact details;</li>
    <li>a statement that you have a good-faith belief the use is not authorised, and that the information in your notice is accurate.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    We review all notices promptly, remove or correct content where a claim is substantiated,
    and will respond to the address you provide. We keep a record of corrections and
    takedowns. Knowingly false or abusive notices may be declined.
  </p>

  <h2>12. Security disclosure</h2>
  <p>
    If you discover a security vulnerability in either site, please report it privately to
